बलाक
Balāka (a hunter). § 608f (Karṇap.) VIII, 69, 3438, 3440, 3441, 3447: The hunter Balāka used to slay animals for the livelihood of his sons and wives, and thus he also supported his parents and others that depended upon him always speaking the truth and never harbouring malice. devoted to the duties of his own order. One day, having found no other animal, he slew a blind (ghrāṇacakshushaṃ) beast of prey while it was drinking water. A shower of flowers fell from the skies; a celestial chariot took him to heaven, while Aps., etc., were singing. That beast of prey had by austerities obtained a boon, and had become the cause of the destruction of all creatures. For this reason he was made blind by Brahman.

