शक्रनमुचिसंवाद
[Śakra-Namuci-saṃvāda(ḥ)], “the discourse between Indra and Namuci.” § 672b (Mokshadh.): When the Asura Namuci, who was conversant with the birth and death of all creatures, was sitting, divested of prosperity but untroubled at heart, bound with cords, brought under the sway of his foes, etc., Indra asked him if he grieved or passed his days cheerfully. Namuci answers that he does not grieve, and explains why, citing Gautama [the spouse of Ahalyā]<BR>(XII, 226).

