कुबेर
Kubera, the lord of riches, regent of the northern quarter, king of the Guhyakas, Rākshasas, and Yakshas, husband of Ṛddhi (Bhadrā). § 155 (Śakuntalop.): I, 74, 3072 (ºsya, sc. bhavanaṃ).--§ 185 (Pāṇḍu): I, 120, 4650 (udyānāni Kºsya, in the north).--§ 221 (Caitrarathap.): I, 170, 6448 (ºsya priyaḥ sakhā, i.e. the Gandharva Citraratha).--§ 238 (Pañcendrop.): I, 197, 7277.--[§ 258 (Khāṇḍavadahanap.): I, 227, 8264 (in the encounter between the gods and Arjuna + Kṛshṇa, Vaiśravaṇa, i.e. K., hurled his mace against Kṛshṇa).]--§ 265 (Lokapālasabhākhyānap.): II, 6, 281.-§ 268 (Varuṇasabhāv.): II, 9, 382 (ºsya sabhāṃ).--[§ 269 (Vaiśravaṇasabhāv.): II, 10 (description of the palace of K., Bhava, i.e. Śiva made friendship with Paulastya, i.e. K. (v. 417)).]--§ 298 (Dyūtap.): II, 58, 1994 (ºbhavanopamaṃ, sc. the palace (rājagṛha) of Yudhishṭhira).--§ 329 (Kāmyakavanapr.): III, 36, 1442 (will give weapons to Arjuna).-§ 334 (Kairātap.): III, 41, 1671 (Yakshair anugataḥ, came to see Arjuna), [1697 (Dhanādhyakshaḥ, gave his weapon Antardhāna to Arjuna)].--§ 356 (Tīrthayātrāp.): III, 80, 3095 (ºena yathā hīnaṃ vanaṃ Caitrarathaṃ yathā).--§ 377 (Dhaumyatīrthayātrāp.): III, 89, 8358 (jajñe Dhanapatir yatra--i.e. on the Narmadā--Kubero Naravāhanaḥ).--§ 378 (Tīrthayātrāp.): III, 91, 8419 (Arjuna had obtained weapons from K.).--§ 418c (Mandara): III, 139, 10824 (Yaksharāṭ, on Mandara).--§ 418 (Tīrthayātrāp.): III, 139, 10829 (ºsacivāḥ…Rākshasāḥ).--§ 418d (Kailāsa): III, 139, 10831 (ºsadanaṃ, on Kailāsa).--§ 420 (Gandhamādanapr.): III, 141, 10894 (ºnalinīṃ ramyāṃ Rākshasair abhisevitāṃ).-§ 432 (Saugandhikāharaṇap.): III, 153, 11352 (ºnalinīṃ …Kºbhavanābhyāse jātāṃ parvatanirjhare), 11358 (ākrīḍaṃ Rājarājasya Kºsya); 154, 11370 (ākrīḍo 'yaṃ Kºsya), 11378 (bhavanaṃ Kºsya, Bhīmasena slew many of the Krodhavaśas, the rest fled to the abode of K.).--§ 433 (do.): III, 155, 11416 (ºsya nalinyāḥ), 11428 (the Pāṇḍavas lived for some time at the lotus-tank of K., with the permission of K.).--[§ 434e (do.): III, 156, 11441 (Vaiśravaṇāvāsaṃ, inhabited by Siddhas).]--§ 438 (Yakshayuddhap.): III, 161, 11747 (ºsadanaṃ), 11753 (do.), 11769 (Yaksharakshogaṇavṛtaṃ), 11770, 11780 (Bhīmasena slew the Yakshas).--[§ 438c (Agastya): III, 161 (K. had been cursed by Agastya, but was freed by Bhīmasena).]--§ 438 (Yakshayuddhap.): III, 162, 11830, 11836 (ºsadanaṃ prati, the Pāṇḍavas lived there for some time).--§ 443 (Nivātakavacayuddhap.): III, 168, 12004 (Naravāhanaḥ, repetition of § 334).--§ 448 (Ājagarap.): III, 176, †12328.--§ 449 (do.): III, 177, †12340 (Kailāsaṃ …Kºkāntaṃ), †12347 (ºkāntāṃ nalinīṃ; c: frequented by gods and Siddhas).--§ 512 (Ghoshayātrāp.): III, 240, 14869 (Citrasena came from Kºbhavanāt).--[§ 525 (Rāmopākhyānap.): III, 274, 15883 foll. (Vaiśravaṇaḥ, son of Pulastya and a cow; he deserted his father, who then assumed the form of Viśravas. Brahmán made K. a god, Dhaneśa (“lord of wealth”) and one of the Lokapālas, and he obtained the friendship of Śiva, a son named Nalakūbara, the vimāna Pushpaka, the sovereignty of the Yakshas, and the state of Rājarāja).]--§ 526 (do.): III, 275, 15890 (Rākshaseśvaraḥ, resided in Laṅkā); [276 (Rāvaṇa conquered Laṅkā and the vimāna of K. (Pushpaka); K. repaired to the Gandhamādana)].--§ 541 (Indrajidvadha): III, 289, 16474 (a Guhyaka came to Vibhīshaṇa from K. from the mountain Śveta).--[§ 543 (Rāmābhishekap.): III, 291, 16600 (Vaiśravaṇāya, having vanquished and slain Rāvaṇa, Rāma returned the vimāna Pushpaka to K.).]--§ 552 (Goharaṇap.): IV, 56, 1770 (came to see the encounter).--§ 555 (Indravijaya): V, 16, †511 (Lokapālaḥ), †515, 518 (g: Indra bestowed the sovereignty of the Yakshas and all the wealth of the world on K.); 18, 545.--§ 556 (Sañjayayānap.): V, 29, †824 (Vaiśravaṇaḥ).--§ 561 (Yānasandhip.): V, 64, 2473 (madhupītakamākshikaṃ…Kºdayitaṃ).--§ 569 (Bhagavadyānap.): V, 139, 4718 (ºsadanaṃ prāpya, sc. the Pāṇḍavas, all. to §§ 433 or 438).--§ 570 (Sainyaniryāṇap.): V, 156, 5289 (ºa iva Yakshāṇāṃ, sc. senāpatiḥ).--§ 571 (Ulūkadūtāgamanap.): V, 162, 5604.--§ 573 (Ambopākhyānap.): V, 192, 7519 (Naravāhanaḥ, cursed Sthūṇa).-§ 574 (Jambūkh.): VI, 6, 217 (enjoys only the fourth part of all wealth).--§ 574e (Gandhamādana): VI, 6, 229 (Guhyakādhipaḥ, together with the Rākshasas on Gandhamādana).--§ 578 (Bhīshmavadhap.): VI, 50, 2039 (gadādharaḥ).--§ 589 (Droṇābhishekap.): VII, 6, 159 (ºa iva Yakshāṇāṃ, sc. senāpatiḥ).--§ 595 (Shoḍaśarāj., v. Rantideva): VII, 67, 2370 (ºsadaneshv api).--§ 595 (Sṛñjaya): VII, 71, 2459 (ºtanayopamaḥ, sc. the son of Sṛñjaya). --§ 596 (Pratijñāp.): VII, 76, 2691; 80, 2846 (ºsya vihāre ca nalinīṃ padmabhūshitāṃ, passed by Kṛshṇa and Arjuna on their way to the abode of Śiva).--§ 600 (Ghaṭotkacavadhap.): VII, 180, 8194.--§ 607 (Karṇap.): VIII, 37, †1737 (Yama-Varuṇa-Kº-Vāsavā vā yadi).--§ 608 (do.): VIII, 92, †4831 (º-Vaivasvata-Vāsavānāṃ tulyaprabhāvāḥ).--§ 615x (Baladevatīrthayātrā): In days of yore, Kubera, the chief of the Yakshas, practised austerities there (i.e. in Kaubera-tīrtha) and obtained many boons: the lordship of all treasures, the friendship of Rudra, the state of a god (suratvaṃ), the state of a Lokapāla, and a son, Nalakūbara. The Maruts installed him. He also obtained a celestial chariot, fleet as thought, yoked with haṃsas, the vimāna Pushpaka, and the lordship of the Nairṛtas (i.e. Rākshasas): IX, 47, 2756 (Yaksharājñā).--§ 621 (Rājadh.): XII, 5, 141 (Arjuna had obtained weapons from K.).--§ 637 (do.): XII, 44, 1525 (ºbhavanaprakhyaṃ, sc. the palace of Durmarshaṇa).--§ 641 (do.): XII, 67, 2521 (ºa iva Nairṛtān, sc. sukhe dhāsyati); [74, (discourse with Mucukunda, who had vanquished his troops)]; 122, 4496 (dhanānāṃ Rākshasānāñ ca Kºm api ceśvaraṃ).--§ 647b (Brahmadatta-Pūjanīsaṃv.): XII, 139, 5239 (ºa iva kāmadaḥ). --§ 730g (Upamanyu): XIII, 14, 912 (sarvayakshāṇāṃ, sc. Śiva).--[§ 731b (Ashṭāvakra-Diksaṃv.): XIII, 19 (visited by Ashṭāvakra).]--§ 746 (Ānuśāsanik.): XIII, 61, 3101 (ºm iva Rakshāṃsi).--§ 778f (Muñjavat): XIV, 8, 183, 186 (ºānucaraiḥ), 190 (ºsya sahāyaiḥ), 191.--§ 782g (Guruśishyasaṃv.): XIV, 43, 1180 (sarvaratnānāṃ rājā).--§ 785 (Anugītāp.): XIV, 65, 1918 (Yakshendrāya).--§ 787 (Āśramavāsap.): XV, 20, 570 (after death Dhṛtarāshṭra will go to Kºbhavanaṃ).--§ 795 (Svargārohaṇap.): XVIII, 5, 175 (ºsya bhavanaṃ). Cf. also the following synonyms:-Alakādhipa, q.v. Dhanada (“wealth-giving”): II, 395, 401, 405, 415, 466, 986, 1011 (uttarāṃ diśaṃ…Dhºpālitāṃ); III, 11287 (ºodyānaṃ), 11322, 11445, 11656, 11666, 11773, 11803, 11829, 11888, †12364; V, 3570, 3831 (on the mountain Kailāsa), 3899, 7532; VII, 1223, 9628; VIII, †678 (ºendrakalpau), †3392 (ºsya lakshmyā, sc. tulyaḥ); IX, 2657 (ṛddhyā Dhºopamāḥ, sc. mātaraḥ); XII, 1528 (Kailāsaṃ Dhºo yathā, sc. labdhvā), (2816), 10052 (Vishṇu identified with Dh.), 10666 (Yaksharakshodhipaḥ), 10668; XIII, 1396(?), 1412 (Mandākinīñ ca nalinīṃ Dhºsya), 1422, 1441. Dhanadeśvara (“wealth-giving lord”): II, 410 (C. by error Daº). Dhanādhigoptṛ (“guardian of treasures”): V, 7521. Dhanādhipa (“lord of treasures”): III, 11766, 11776, 11779; XIII, 1432. Dhanādhipati (do.): III, 11768, 11837, 11838; IX, 2757. Dhanādhyaksha (“overseer of treasures”): I, 6837, 7581 (Kauravasya niveśanaṃ Dhºkshayopamaṃ); III, 1697, 11831, 16177; X, 498 (ºopamaṃ); XIV, 1923. Dhanānām īśvaraḥ (“lord of treasures”): I, 408, 409, 414; II, 1672. Dhanapati (do.): I, 7853 (ishṭā Dhºeḥ, sc. Vargā); II, 417 (ºeḥ sakhā, i.e. Śiva), 481, 991 (diśaṃ Dhºeḥ); III, 8358 (Kº); V, 3898(?); VIII, 1998 (sagadāt…api); XII, 10667. Dhaneśa (do.): III, 14546, 15886 (ºtvaṃ); VII, 2522; XVIII, 160 (ºsya lokān). Dhaneśvara (do.): I, †7010, 8264 (hurled his mace against Kṛshṇa); II, 407, 417, 418; III, 770 (devagaṇāḥ …saha-Dhºāḥ), 10224, 11372, 11375, 11392, 11394, 11722, 11757, 11761, 11772, 11775, (11792), 11799, 11933 (ºgṛhaº), †15590, 15920, 15925, 16189; IV, 2281 (ºam ivāmarāḥ); V, 545 (Kº), 3968 (reme…yathā carddhyāṃ Dhºaḥ), 7481 (ºsyānucaraḥ, sc. Sthūṇa); VI, 4902 (sagadaḥ); VII, 1844 (ºsutopamaṃ, sc