Nadīja = Bhīshma, q.v.

नाडीजङ्घ
Nāḍījaṅgha, a crane (= Rājadharman), son of Kaśyapa. § 468 (Indradyumnop.): III, 199, ††13337.--§ 658b (Kṛtaghnop.): XII, 169, 6336 (Brahmaṇaḥ sakhā bakarājo mahāprājñaḥ Kaśyapasyātmasambhavaḥ).

नदीसुत
Nadīsuta (“the son of the river [i.e. Gangā]”) = Bhīshma: XII, 807; XIII, 7696.

नाद्योपहारलुब्ध
Nādyopahāralubdha = Śiva (1000 names^1).

नाग
Nāga, pl. (ºāḥ) (“Serpents”), sons of Kaśyapa and Kadrū (or Surasā). § 11 (Anukram.): I, 2, 365 (sarva-Nºānāṃ …sambhavaḥ), 369 (ºānāṃ…samudbhavaḥ).--§ 17 (Uttaṅka): I, 3, 797 (Airāvatarājānaḥ sarpāḥ), 799 (bahūni Nºveśmāni Gaṅgāyās tīra uttare), 804 (prārthayan Nºmukhyatāṃ, sc. Śrutasena), ††806, ††813, ††830 (ºbhavane, the adventures of Uttaṅka in the world of the N.).--§ 26 (Jaratkāru): I, 15, 1063 (all. to § 68).--§ 27 (Kaśyapa): I, 16, 1076 (ºsahasraṃ, sons of Kaśyapa and Kadrū).-[§ 29 (Kadrū): I, 20 (Kadrū cursed her sons, that they should be consumed by the fire at the snake-sacrifice of Janamejaya).]--§ 30b (Samudra): I, 21, 1210 (ºānām ālayaṃ, i.e. the ocean).--§ 31 (Kadrū): I, 22, 1223.--§ 31b (Samudradarśana): I, 22, 1230 (do.).--§ 32b (Garuḍa): I, 23, 1247 (ºkshayakaraḥ, i.e. Garuḍa).--§ 36 (Kadrū): I, 25, 1282 (ºānām ālayaḥ, i.e. the ocean).--§ 38 (do.): I, 26, 1301.--§ 39 (Rāmaṇīyaka): I, 27, 1304, 1310.-§ 40 (Garuḍa): I, 28, 1332.--§ 47 (Sarpanāmak.): I, 35, 1562 (enumeration of the principal serpents).--§ 49 (Vāsuki): I, 37, 1606, 1608, 1614; 38, 1637, 1640; 39, 1641 (consultation of the serpents about the means of being delivered from the curse of Kadrū).--§ 51 (Parikshit): I, 43, 1792, 1795.--§ 58b foll. (Sarpasattra): I, 53, 2052, 2053, 2059 (the snake-sacrifice of Janamejaya, Āstīka obtained from Janamejaya the boon that the sacrifice might be ended).--§ 63 (do.): I, 57, 2148 (enumeration of the serpents of Vāsuki's race who had fallen into the fire).-§ 64 (do.): I, 57, 2151 (do., of Takshaka's race).--§ 65 (do.): I, 57, 2153 (do., of Airāvata's race).--§ 66 (do.): I, 57, 2155 (do., of Dhṛtarāshṭra's race), 2160.--[§ 68 (Āstīka): I, 58 (thus Āstīka had delivered the serpents from the curse of Kadrū).]--§ 129 (Aṃśāvat.): I, 66, 2634 (sons of Surasā, the pannagas being sons of Kadrū).-§ 191 (Arjuna): I, 123, 4829 (present at the birth of Arjuna, enumeration).--§ 197 (Bhīmasenarasap.): I, 128, 5018 (ºbhavane, ºkumārakān), 5025, 5031, 5033; 129, 5057, 5058, 5060 (Bhīmasena was rescued by the Nāgas and dwelt for some time in the world of the Nāgas).--§ 233 (Svayaṃvarap.): I, 187, 7017 (Suparṇa-Nºāsura-Siddhajushṭaṃ, sc. antarīkshaṃ).--§ 244 (Rājyalābhap.): I, 207, 7575 (ºair Bhogavatī yathā), 7594 (do.).--§ 246 (Sundopasundop.): I, 210, 7659 (antarbhūmigatān, vanquished by Sunda and Upasunda); 212, 7712 (Sunda and Upasunda robbed the jewels of the N.).--§ 247 (Arjunavanavāsap.): I, 213, 7745 (ºair iva Sarasvatī).--§ 256b (Khāṇḍavadahanap.): I, 224, 8171.--§ 257d (Sudarśana): I, 225, 8198 (ºānām adhikaḥ, sc. Kṛshṇa).--§ 258 (Khāṇḍavadahanap.): I, 228, 8285, 8318 (slain at the conflagration of the Khāṇḍava forest).--§ 267 (Yamasabhāv.): II, 8, 335 (śataṃ, in the palace of Yama).--[§ 268 (Varuṇasabhāv.): II, 9, 363 (sarpāḥ, enumeration).]--§ 270 (Brahmasabhāv.): II, 11, 465 (worship Brahmán), 472 (come to the palace of Brahmán).--§ 271 (Lokapālasabhākh.): II, 12, 480 (in the palace of Varuṇa).--§ 314 (Āraṇyakap.): III, 9, 330.--§ 345 (Nalopākhyānap.): III, 57, 2195 (ºair Bhogavatīm iva).--§ 362 (Tīrthayātrāp.): III, 83, 5084 (Sarpadevīṃ…Nºānāṃ tīrthaṃ).--§ 373 (Prayāga): III, 85, 8214 (in Prayāga).--§ 418d (Kailāsa): III, 139, 10831 (on the mountain of Kailāsa).--§ 437 (Yakshayuddhap.): III, 160, 11696 (º-mahānāga-º).--§ 445 (Nivātakavacayuddhap.): III, 173, 12262 (hatanāgam iva hradaṃ).-§ 459 (Mārkaṇḍeyas.): III, 188, 12924, 12942 (seen by Mārkaṇḍeya in the stomach of Nārāyaṇa).--§ 460b (Kaliyuga): III, 190, 13075 (ºānām ālayeshu), 13093.-§ 476 (Dhundhumārop.): III, 202, 13535 (ºānāṃ… varaṃ).--§ 549d (Dhṛtarāshṭra): IV, 2, 44 (Dhṛtarāshṭraś ca Nºānāṃ, sc. varaḥ).--§ 552 (Goharaṇap.): IV, 26, 887 (iva Nºendrāḥ?, “elephants” PCR.).--§ 561 (Yānasandhip.): V, 71, †2580 (º-Rakshasāṃ, sc. janitraṃ, i.e. Kṛshṇa (Vishṇu).-§ 564 (Mātaliyop.): V, 97, 3520 (M. would seek a husband for his daughter among the N.); 103, 3620 (Surasāyāḥ sutāḥ), 3624 (enumeration of the principal Nāgas).--§ 565 (Gālavacarita): V, 120, 4028 (º-Yaksha-manushyānāṃ samāgamaḥ).--§ 573 (Ambopākhyānap.): V, 179, 7152 (ksharanto rudhiraṃ Nºā iva).--§ 574 (Jambūkh.): VI, 6, 246 (sarpā Nºāś ca, on the mountain Nishada).--§ 576 (Bhagavadgītāp.): VI, 34, 1233 (Anantaś cāsmi Nºānāṃ, says Kṛshṇa).-§ 586 (Bhīshmavadhap.): VI, 104, 4758 (mahāśaktiṃ… Nāgakanyopamāṃ).--§ 600 (Ghaṭotkacavadhap.): VII, 156, †6921 (applauded Aśvatthāman); 163, †7293.--§ 601 (Droṇavadhap.): VII, 185, 8434 (had been slain by Arjuna in the Khāṇḍava forest, cf. § 258).--§ 603b (Nārāyaṇa): VII, 201, †9465 (º-Rakshaḥ-Piśācān, 9475.--§ 605 (Karṇap.): VIII, 24, 925 (āśīvishā yathā Nºā bhittvā gām salilaṃ yayuḥ), 975 (only C., read nagāḥ (“mountains”) with B.).--§ 606 (Tripurākhyāna): VIII, 34, 1483 (enumeration, became the vālabandhanāḥ of the horses of Śiva).--§ 608 (Karṇap.): VIII, 87, 4415 (sided with Arjuna), 4426 (º-Yakshāḥ); 88, †4490 (deva-NºāsuraSiddha-Yakshāḥ).--§ 611 (Śalyap.): IX, 10, 498 (lelihānām iva…Nāgakanyāṃ, sc. śaktiṃ).--§ 615 (Gadāyuddhap.): IX, 58, 3271 (Garutmantau yathā Nºāmishaishiṇau); 62, 3464 (hṛtanāgam iva hradaṃ).--§ 616 (Sauptikap.): X, 6, 219 (º-yajñopavītinaṃ, sc. Aśvatthāman).--§ 617 (Aishīkap.): X, 15, 715.--§ 618 (Jalapradānik.): XI, 5, 133 (pañcaśīrshadharaiḥ).--§ 637 (Rājadh.): XII, 47, 1636 (paryaṅke Nºbhūshite, sc. Kṛshṇa's).--§ 641f (Pṛthu Vainya): XII, 59, 2245 (Yaksha-Rākshasa-Nºaiḥ).--§ 660b (BhṛguBharadvājasaṃv.): XII, 188, 6932 (Yaksha-RākshasaNºāḥ).--§ 662b (Jāpakop.): XII, 200, 7341.--§ 667 (Mokshadh.): XII, 210, 7657.--§ 704 (do.): XII, 301, †11097.--§ 717b (Nārāyaṇīya): XII, 344, 13366.-§ 718b (Uñchavṛttyup.): XII, 361, 13866 (Saurabheyāḥ, read Sauraseyāḥ).--§ 723 (Ānuśāsanik.): XIII, 6, 308.-§ 730g (Upamanyu): XIII, 14, 746 (nāgamauñjī, etc., sc. Śiva).--§ 734 (do.): XIII, 26, 1807 (sudhā yathā ca Nºānāṃ).--§ 746 (do.): XIII, 67, 3388 (ºānāñ ca sudhā tathā, sc. annaṃ).--§ 747 (do.): XIII, 84, 3978 (kuñjarāḥ smṛtā Nºāḥ).--§ 747b (Suvarṇotpatti): XIII, 85, 4023.-§ 754 (Ānuśāsanik.): XIII, 98, 4705 (Gandharva-NºYakshebhyaḥ), 4711, 4738 (ºānāṃ dayitā nityaṃ padmotpalavimiśritāḥ, sc. balayaḥ).--§ 759 (do.): XIII, 107, 5261 (ºvīthīsamākulaṃ, sc. vimānaṃ).--§ 766 (do.): XIII, 132, 6163 (Baladevaprabhṛtayaḥ), 6171.--§ 768b (UmāMaheśvaras.): XIII, 140, 6351; 142, 6544.--§ 768c (Balarāma): XIII, 147, 6862.--§ 782b (Brāhmaṇagītā): XIV, 26, 752.--§ 782g (Guruśishyas.): XIV, 43, 1183 (deva-Dānava-Nºānāṃ…īśvaraḥ, i.e. Vishṇu).--§ 784b (Uttaṅka): XIV, 54, 1566, 1581 (ºyonau, ºvat); 57, 1685 (deva-Rākshasa-Nºānāṃ); 58, 1740 (Vāsukipramukhānāṃ), 1744, 1746, 1748 (Vāsukipramukhānāṃ) (Uttaṅka's adventures in the world of the N.).--§ 793 (Mausalap): XVI, 4, †118 (the N. (enumeration) welcomed the serpent who after the death of Balarāma issued from his body). Cf. Kādraveya, pl., Kadrūja, pl., and also pannaga, pl., sarpa, pl.--Names of single Nāgas:-Aśvasena: I, 8242; VIII, †4633 (Aº), †4676, †4677 (º-rāja), †4679. Aśvatara: II, 361 (Kambalāśvatarau nāgau). Airāvaṇa: II, 360 (Aº). Airāvata: V, 3627 (Aº). Akarkara: I, 1561 (Karkarākarkarau nāgau). Anīla: I, 1552 (Nīlānīlau nāgau). Apūraṇa: I, 1551. Āryaka: I, 5028, 5031. Atishaṇḍa: XVI, †120 (Cakramandātishaṇḍau). Bahumūlaka: I, 1561 (Bº). Baladeva: XVI, †117{??} Cakramanda: XVI, †120 (Cakramandātishaṇḍau). Cikura: V, 3640 (Cº). Śaṅkhamukha: I, 1556 (Śº). Śesha: I, 1586, 1587, 2786; III, 13557 (Śº), 13562 (ºbhoge); V, 3618 (Śº); VII, 9563 (ºendra, Śº). Dhṛtarāshṭra: XVI, †119 (Dº). Elāpatra: I, 1648 (Eº). Jaya: IX, 2554 (Jº). Kalaśapotaka: I, 1552. Kālīyaka: I, 1555 (Kº). Kambala: II, 361 (Kambalāśvatarau nāgau). Kapila: III, 8010 (º-rāja, Kº). Kardama: I, 1561 (Kº). Karkara: I, 1561 (Karkarākarkarau nāgau). Karkoṭaka: III, 2610 (ºrājan), 2611 (Kº), 2616 (ºendra), 2617 (Kº), 2620, 2621 (Kº), 2633 (ºrāja), 2634 2843 (do.), 2997 (do.), 3072 (Kº). Kauravya: I, 7788 (ºrājasya kanyayā, i.e. Ulūpī), 7789 (Kº), 7791 (ºrājasutā, i.e. Ulūpī), 7809 (ºbhavane); XIV, 2371 (nāgottamasutā, i.e. Ulūpī), 2383 (ºrājapateḥ sutā, i.e. Ulūpī), 2399 (ºendraduhitar, i.e. Ulūpī), 2420 (ºendra). Kshemaka: I, 1556 (Kº). Mahājaya: IX, 2554 (Mº). Maṇi: V, 3626. Nahusha^2: III, 12402 (N. as ajagara), 12471 (ºendra), 12486 (do.). Nīla: I (Nīlānīlau nāgau). Padma: XII, 13803 (padmanābho mahānāgaḥ Padma ity eva viśrutaḥ), 13807, 13823, 13824, 13825 (ºpatnī), 13827, (13829) (ºbhāryā), 13836, 13844, (13852), 13854 (ºendra), 13857 (do.), 13862 (do.), (13864), (13868) (ºbhāryā), (13876), 13885 (ºendra), 13888, (13891), 13892, (13901), (13927). Piṇḍāraka: I, 1556 (Pº). Piñjaraka: I, 1551. Prabhākara: I, 1560. Reṇuka: XIII, 6156 (Rº). Sumukha: V, 3639 (Sº), 3642, 3674. Takshaka: I, ††774 (ºrāja, Tº), ††775 (do.), 802 (do.), 1760 (ºendra, Tº), 1768 (ºendra), 1769 (do.), 1771, 1789 (Tº), 1803, 1979 (ºendra, Tº), 1983, 1987, 2054 (ºendra, Tº), 2056 (ºendra), 2123, 2133, 2134 (do., Tº), 2167, 8236 (ºrāja, Tº); III, 5032 (Tº); V, 3798 (Tº). Vāsuki: I, 1125 (ºrājo, C., but read with B. ºrājño), 1126, 1127 (Vº), 1644 (Vº), 1647 (ºrāj), 1649 (ºendra), 1652 (do.), 1865 (do.), 1866 (do.), 1872, 1878 (svasā Nºpateḥ, i.e. Jaratkāru), 1924 (do.), 2078 (ºrāja), 2085, 2088 (ºendra), 5023 (do.), 5027 (do., Vº), 5029 (do.), 5056; IX, 2518 (ºrāja, Vº). Vegavat: I, 2158 (Vº).

नाग
Nāga, adj. (“belonging to the Serpents”). § 608 (Karṇap.): VII, 53, 2586 (ºm astraṃ, employed by Arjuna).

नागशत
Nāgaśata, a mountain. § 184 (Pāṇḍu): I, 119, 4636 (giriṃ, in the north, visited by Pāṇḍu).

नागाशिन्
Nāgāśin, a Suparṇa, son of Garuḍa. § 564 (Mātalīyop.): V, 101, 3594 (enumeration).

नागदत्त
Nāgadatta, a son of Dhṛtarāshṭra. § 130 (Aṃśāvat.): I, 67, 2737.--§ 182 (Dhṛtarāshṭraputranāmak.): I, 117, 4550.--§ 600 (Ghaṭotkacavadhap.): VII, 157, 6938 (among ten sons of Dhṛtarāshṭra who are slain by Bhīmasena).

नागधन्वन्
Nāgadhanvan (Nāgavatman, C.), a tīrtha on the Sarasvatī. § 615g (Baladevatīrthayātrā): N. is swarming with numerous snakes, it was the abode of Vāsuki and of 14,000 Ṛshis; there is no fear of snakes. There the gods had installed Vāsuki as king of all the snakes: IX, 37, 2148.

नागद्वीप
Nāgadvīpa, a region. § 574 (Jambūkh.): VI, 6, 251.

नागाह्वय
Nāgāhvaya^1 (“named after the elephant”) = Hāstinapura: VII, 8 (puraṃ); XIV, 1933 (puraṃ), 2510 (puraṃ).

नागाह्वय
Nāgāhvaya^2, a city of the Nāgas (Serpents) on the Gomatī. § 718b (Uñchavṛttyup.): XII, 356, 13801 (puraṃ).

नागलोक
Nāgaloka (“the world of the Nāgas (Serpents)”). § 17 (Uttaṅka): I, 3, ††793, ††796, ††815, ††823.--§ 197 (Bhīmasenarasapāna): I, 129, 5059, 5065.--§ 360 (Tīrthayātrāp.): III, 82, 5055.--§ 362 (do.): III, 83, 5085.-§ 458 (Mārkaṇḍeyas.): III, 188, 12875.--§ 524c (Vishṇu): III, 272, 15810 (pl.).--§ 564 (Mātalīyop.): V, 97, 3519; 98, 3529; 99, 3547 (ºsya nābhishṭhāne…Pātālaṃ); 102, 3616, 3620.--§ 585b (Irāvat): VI, 90, 3980 (ºe saṃvṛddhaḥ, sc. Irāvat).--§ 784c (Uttaṅka): The N. extended thousand of yojanas on all sides, and had many walls of pure gold, and was decked with jewels and gems; there were many fine tanks of water with flights of staircases made of pure crystal, and many rivers of clear and transparent water; Uttaṅka also saw many trees with diverse species of birds; the gate was five yojanas high and 100 yojanas in width: XIV, 58, 1719, 1722, 1723, 1725, 1726, 1730, 1738, 1739.

नागपति
Nāgapati, pl. (ºayaḥ) (“lords of the Serpents”). § 606 (Tripurākhyāna): VIII, 34, 1481 (daśa Nºīn…Dhṛtarāshṭramukhān, Śiva made them the īshā of his chariot).

नागपुर
Nāgapura, name of a town = Hāstinapura: I, 4464 (ºālayāt), 4632, 4907, 5147, 5207; III, 8456, †12591, †12592; V, 4960, 4961; VI, †781 (only B.); VIII, 17, 25; IX, 1465; XIV, †1531.

नागपुराधिप
Nāgapurādhipa (“lord of Nāgapura”) = Pāṇḍu: I, 4459.

नागपुरसिंह
Nāgapurasiṃha (do.) = Pāṇḍu: I, 4462.

नागसाह्वय
Nāgasāhvaya (“named after the elephant”) = Hāstinapura: I, 506 (nagaraṃ), 1786 (do.), 2249 (nagarāt), 5146 (nagaraṃ), 6343 (do.); II, 1663 (nagare); III, 35 (do.), 890, 15258, 15265 (do.); V, 127 (nagaraṃ), 129 (do.), 5674 (pure); IX, 3499, 3536; XI, 313: XII, 128 (nagaraṃ), 1386 (do.); XIII, 7703 (do.); XIV, 1856 (nagare), 2047, 2065 (nagaraṃ); XVI, 291 (do.).

नागतीर्थ
Nāgatīrtha, a tīrtha. § 370 (Tīrthayātrāp.): III, 84, 8011.

नगात्मजा
Nagātmajā (“daughter of the Mountain [i.e. Himavat]”) = Umā: XIII, 6382.

नागवत्मन्
Nāgavatman, v. Nāgadhanvan.

नागेन्द्र
Nāgendra = Śiva (1000 names^1). 

नागी
*nāgī (“female serpent”): I, 8239 (= the wife of Takshaka and mother of Aśvasena); XII, 13834 (= the wife of Padma).

नग्नजित्
Nagnajit, a king [of the Gandhāras?]. § 83 (Ādivaṃśāvatāraṇa): I, 63, 2455 (Prahlādaśishyo Nºt Subalaś cābhavat tataḥ, is not N. another name of Subala?).--§ 130 (Aṃśāvat.): I, 67, 2657 (incarnation of the Asura Ishupa (Ishupad, B.)). --§ 515 (Karṇadigvijaya): III, 254, 15257 (ºpramukhān gaṇān, vanquished by Karṇa on his digvijaya).--§ 561c (Kṛshṇa Vāsudeva): V, 48, 1882 (ayaṃ--i.e. Kṛshṇa-Gāndhārāṃs tarasā sampramathyajitvā putrān Nºaḥ samagrān| baddhaṃ mumoca vinadantaṃ prasahya Sudarśanaṃ vai devatānāṃ lalāmaṃ).--§ 589 (Droṇābhishekap.): VII, 4, 120 (ºpramukhā nṛpāḥ, had been vanquished by Karṇa)

नग्नजित
Nagnajita, pl. (ºāḥ), a people or a family (“the sons of Nagnajit”?). § 608 (Karṇap.): VIII, 79, 4040 (had been vanquished by Karṇa, BR. conjectures Nāº).

नागोद्भेद
Nāgodbheda, a tīrtha. § 360 (Tīrthayātrāp.): III, 82, 5053, 5055 (there the Sarasvatī reappears).

नहुष
Nahusha^1, a serpent. § 47 (Sarpanāmak.): I, 35, 1554 (enumeration).--§ 564 (Mātalīyop.): V, 103, 3625 (do.).-§ 606 (Tripurākhyāna): VIII, 34, 1483 (among the serpents who became the vālabandhanāḥ of the horses of Śiva). Cf. Nahusha^2.

नहुष
Nahusha^2, an ancient king, son of Āyus and father of Yayāti, etc., for some time king of the gods, but cursed and born as a serpent (ajagara). § 141 (Purūravas): I, 75, 3150 (eldest son of Āyus and Svarbhānavī).--§ 142 (cf. Purūravas): N. protected the Pitṛs, gods, Ṛshis, brahmans, Gandharvas, Uragas, Rākshasas, etc.; having slain the Dasyus, he made the Ṛshis pay tribute and carry him on their back like beasts of burden, and having conquered the gods, he became Indra: I, 75, 3151 (Āyushaḥ putraḥ), 3152.-§ 143: N. had six sons: Yati, Yayāti, Saṃyāti, Ayāti, Ayati, and Dhruva. Yati betaking himself to asceticism, Yayāti became the successor of N. and begot with Devayānī, Yadu and Turvasu, and with Śārmishṭhā, Druhyu, Anu, and Pūru: I, 75, 3155.--§ 149 (Yayāti): I, 88, †3564 (ºsya putra, i.e. Yayāti), †3577 (Yayātir Nºsya putraḥ); 93, †3684 (Yayātiḥ…Nºsya putraḥ).--§ 156 (Pūruvaṃś.): I, 95, ††3760 (son of Āyus and father of Yayāti).--§ 267 (Yamasabhāv.): II, 8, 319 (in the palace of Yama).--§ 296 (Dyūtap.): II, 53, 1930.--§ 310 (Āraṇyakap.): III, 3, 141. --§ 384 (Agastyop.): III, 103, 8777 (had oppressed the gods who were rescued by Agastya, cf. § 555).--§ 450 (Ājagarap.): III, 179, 12408 (rājarshiḥ), 180, 12453 (do.); 12460 (rājā); 181, 12533 (nṛpatiḥ, cursed by Agastya (cf. § 555), N. became a serpent (ajagara), but was liberated by Yudhishṭhira).--§ 516 (Duryodhanayajña): III, 257, 15329 (had performed the Vaishṇava sacrifice).--§ 552 (Goharaṇap.): IV, 56, 1768 (came to see the encounter).-§ 555 (Indravijaya): V, 11, 342, 344, 354, 357, 361, 366, 367; 12, 368, 379, 380, 382, 392, 393, 397, 398, 399, 400; 13, 401 (Devarāj), 402, 404 (Devarājaṃ), 406, (407), 408, 415, 421; 14, 441, 444; 15, 446, 448, 450, 451, 461, 470, 472; 16, 505, 506, †508, †509, †510, †513, †514, †515, †516; 17, 520, 522, 526, 527, 528, 529, 530, 542 (N. became the king of the gods, but cursed by Agastya he fell down and became a serpent for 10,000 years).--§ 555 (Sainyodyogap.): V, 17, 556.--§ 562 (Bhagavadyānap.): V, 90, 3146 (worshipped Kṛshṇa).--§ 576 (Bhagavadgītāp.): VI, 17, 645.--§ 599e (Śini): VII, 144, 6029 (son of Āyus and father of Yayāti).--§ 621 (Rājadh.): XII, 8, 211 (a sentence of his is quoted), (), 238 (the earth had belonged to N.).- § 656 (Khaḍgotpattik.): XII, 166, 6193, 6194 (received the sword from Āyus, from N. it passed over to Yayāti).--§ 680b (Tulādhāra-Jājalisaṃv.): XII, 263, 9388, 9389, 9390 (N. had slain a cow and a bull and was rebuked by the ṛshis, who however distributed the sin incurred among the creatures in the form of diseases).--§ 684 (Mokshadh): XII, 269, 9601 (āmnāyam anupaśyan hi purāṇaṃ śāśvataṃ dhruvaṃ | Nºḥ pūrvam ālebhe Tvashṭur gāṃ, cf. § 680b).--§ 717b (Nārāyaṇīya): XII, 343, VII), ††13214 (Āyushaḥ putraṃ), ††13216 (repetition from § 555).--§ 745 (Ānuśāsanik.): XIII, 50, 2642 (ºsya saṃvādaṃ maharsheś Cyavanasya ca).-§ 745b (Cyavanop.): XIII, 50, 2667; 51, 2668, (2671), (2673), (2675), (2677), (2679), 2681, 2682, (2685), 2688, 2690, (2692), 2708, 2710, 2713 (how Cyavana was purchased by N. at the price of a cow).--§ 746 (Ānuśāsanik.): XIII, 81, 3806 (had made gifts of kine).--§ 751b (Śapathavidhi): XIII, 94, 4551, (4574).--§ 755b (Nahushop.): XIII, 99, 4745 (ºsya ca saṃvādam Agastyasya Bhṛgos tathā), 4746, 4747, 4757 (Devendrasya), 4764, 4770; 100, 4782, 4790, 4792, 4795, 4797, 4803, 4804, 4805, 4809, 4810 (the story of N.'s becoming the king of the gods and falling from that place (somewhat different from the narration § 555) and allusion to § 450).--§ 761 (Ānuśāsanik.): XIII, 115, 5662 (abstained from meat during the month of Kārttika).-§ 768b (Kṛshṇa Vāsudeva): XIII, 147, 6832 (son of Āyus and father of Yayāti).--§ 775 (Ānuśāsanik.): XIII, 166, 7674 (enumeration). Cf. also the following synonyms:-Devarāj (“king of the gods”): V, 401; XIII, 4765, 4788, 4793. Devarāja (do.): V, 354 (Nº), 369, 371, 380 (Nº), 404 (Nº), 455. Devarājan (do.): V, 368. Devendra (do.): V, 356; XIII, 4757 (Nº), 4767 (kuº), 4775 (do.). Jagatpati, q.v. Nāga, Nāgendra, v. Nāga, pl. Surādhipa (“lord of the gods”): V, 372, 454, 457; XIII, 4790. Surapati (do.): XIII, 4789. Sureśvara (do.), q.v. Surendra (do.): V, 461; XIII, 4782 (ºtvaṃ), 4784.

नहुष
Nahusha^3 = Kṛshṇa (Vishṇu): XII, 1511; XIII, 6983 (Vishṇu's 1000 names).

नाहुष
Nāhusha, Nahushātmaja = Yayāti, q.v.

नहुषोपाख्यान
[Nahushopākhyāna]<BR>(“the episode relating to Nahusha”). § 755b (Ānuśāsanik.): Nahusha, having acquired the sovereignty of heaven, performed both human and celestial acts, for instance bali and offering of incense and of light, etc.; and although he had become the chief of the gods, he yet worshipped the gods as in days of yore. But then he was filled with pride in consequence of the boon he had received from all the gods, and he ceased to perform religious actions; for a very long time he used to employ the Ṛ. by turns as the bearers of his vehicles; but in consequence of his abstention from religious acts his energy began to diminish; when it was Agastya's turn to carry the vehicle, Bhṛgu came to the hermitage of Agastya (Maitrāvaruṇi), and when the latter, mentioning Brahmán's boon to Nahusha (that whoever would come within the range of his eyesight would, deprived of all energy, be within his sway), and that Brahmán had given him aṃṛta to drink, despaired of being able to curse him, Bhṛgu said that he had come at the command of Brahmán, and that he would curse Nahusha to be a snake, when he insulted Agastya by a kick, and this very day he would hurl him down and re-establish Indra. Agastya became highly gratified (XIII, 99). Asked by Yudhishṭhira, Bhīshma continued [referring to and developing the beginning of XIII, 99: Nahusha's sacrificial rites and presents were obstructed by Rā.]: Nahusha ordered Ṛ. Agastya to carry the vehicle from the banks of Sarasvatī. Bhṛgu, telling Agastya to close his eyes, had entered the matted locks of Agastya, taking care not to cast his eyes upon Nahusha. Nahusha urged Agastya on with his goad and then struck him on the head with his left foot. Then Nahusha was cursed by Bhṛgu and transformed into a snake, but, in consequence of his gifts, etc., he retained his memory, and, assisted by Agastya, pacified Bhṛgu, who said that Yudhishṭhira would rescue him, and then went to the abode of Brahmán and informed him, while Agastya returned to his own hermitage. Brahmán once more installed (caused the gods to instal) Indra in the sovereignty of heaven. “Thou hast, O king, rescued Nahusha from Bhṛgu's curse; he ascended to the abode of Brahmán before thy eyes; in consequence of the merits he had acquired through acts of the kind I have mentioned, he succeeded in once more regaining his lost position. Hence, when evening comes, persons leading the domestic mode of life, should give lamps” (XIII, 100).

नैगमेय
Naigameya, a god associated to or a form of Skanda. § 116 (Vasu, pl.): I, 66, 2588 (son(?) (brother, PCR.) of Skanda, tasya ŚākhoViśākhaś ca Nºś ca pṛshṭhajaḥ).-§ 498 (Skandotpatti): III, 226, 14367 (Agnir bhūtvā Nºś chāgavaktro bahuprajo ramayām āsa śailasthaṃ--sc. Skanda--bālaṃ krīḍanakair iva).--§ 508 (Kārttikeyastava): III, 232, 14634 (among the names of Skanda).--§ 615u (Skanda): IX, 44, 2487 (one of the four forms of Skanda, tasya Śākho Viśakhaś ca Nºaś ca pṛshṭhataḥ), 2489 (kumāro pāvakaprabhuḥ).

