वृत्र
Vṛtra, an Asura, slain by Indra. § 17 (Uttaṅka): I, 3, †810 (ºsya hantā, i.e. Indra).--§ 96 (Aṃśāvat.): I, 65, 2541 (mahāsuraḥ, son of Danāyus).--§ 130 (do.): I, 67, 2680 (mahāsuraḥ, incarnate as king Maṇimat).--§ 221 (Caitrarathap.): I, 170, 6485 (the thunderbolt of Indra (vajraṃ Vºnibarhaṇam) broke into thousand pieces on the head of V.).-§ 277 (Jarāsandhavadhap.): II, 23, 914 (º-Vāsavayor iva, sc. saṃyuge).--§ 317 (Arjunābhigamanap.): III, 12, 568 (º-Vāsavayor iva, sc. yuddhaṃ).--§ 320 (Saubhavadhop.): III, 16, 681 (º-Vāsavayoḥ…yathā, sc. yuddhaṃ).--§ 330 (Indradarśana): III, 37, 1469 (from fear of V. the gods imparted all their might to Indra).--§ 331 (Kairātap.): III, 39, 1608 (º-Vāsavayor iva, sc. yuddhaṃ).--§ 384 (Agastyop.): III, 100, 8692, 8693 (ºvadhe), 8695, 8699 (headed by V. the Kālakeyas defeated the gods; Tvashṭr then constructed the thunderbolt (vajra) for Indra); 101, 8714, 8721, †8726, †8730 (ºvadhaº, Indra slew V. with the vajra); 103, 8769, 8770 (nihataṃ).--§ 402 (Tīrthayātrāp.): III, 120, †10264 (jahi…Vºṃ yathā Devapatiḥ).--§ 413 (do.): III, 135, 10693 (ºṃ hatvā Śacīpatiḥ).--§ 443 (Nivātakavacayuddhap.): III, 168, 12073 (Bala-Vºau, vanquished by Indra).--§ 444 (do.): 171, 12150 (ºsya vadhe).--§ 539 (Kumbhakarṇādivadha): III, 287, 16428 (ºsaṅkāśaṃ Kumbhakarṇaṃ).-§ 544 (Yudhishṭhirāśvāsa): III, 292, 16605 (nihataḥ).-§ 550 (Samayapālanap.): IV, 10, 356 (º-Vāsavayor iva, sc. saṃyuge).--§ 551 (Kīcakavadhap.): IV, 22, 727 (hanishyāmi Vºṃ Devapatir yathā).--§ 552 (Goharaṇap.): IV, 58, 1868 (º-Vāsavayor iva, sc. yuddhaṃ); 59, 1903 (º-Vāsavayor iva, kiratoḥ śarajālāni).--§ 555 (Indravijaya): V, 9, 275, 277, 279 (º-Vāsavayoḥ, sc. yuddhaṃ), 280, 281, 282 (Jṛmbhikāṃ Vºnāśinīṃ), 284 (º-Vāsavayoḥ, sc. yuddhaṃ), 285, 289 (ºvadhepsavaḥ) (Tvashṭṛ created V. who assailed Indra); 10, 290, 296, 299, 303, 306, 307, 309, 310, 322, 323, 326, 327, 329, 330, 331, 334 (hate) (notwithstanding the compact with V, Indra wilily slew him); 13, 412 (tvadvīryanihate); 16, 500 (hataḥ), 504, †512 (nihataḥ); 17, 522 (ºāsuravadhena).-§ 555 (Sainyodyogap.): V, 18, 555 (ºṃ hatvā yathā… Śakraḥ).--§ 565 (Gālavacarita): V, 109, 3791 (atra--i.e. in the south--vṛttena Vºo 'pi Śakraśatrutvam īyivān).--§ 568 (Vidulāputraśāsana): V, 134, 4562 (Indro Vºvadhenaiva Mahendraḥ samapadyata).--§ 573 (Ambopākhyānap.): V, 177, 7024 (jahi…yathā Vºṃ Purandaraḥ).--§ 578 (Bhīshmavadhap.): VI, 45, 1706 (Vṛtra-Vāsavayor iva, only B.); 48, 1913 (yathā vā Vº-Vāsavau, sc. ayudhyetāṃ).-§ 584 (do.): VI, 84, 3721 (ºsyeva Purandaraḥ, sc. vadhā kaṅkshī).--§ 585 (do.): VI, 90, 4029 (yathā vai Vº-Vāsavau, sc. saṅgrāme vyavatishṭhetāṃ).--§ 586 (do.): VI, 100, 4553 (ºo devacamūṃ iva, sc. dravayate), 4580 (º-Vāsavayoḥ, sc. yuddhaṃ).--§ 587 (do.): VI, 110, 5138 (yathā Vºṃ Purandaraḥ, sc. avārayat); 111, 5183 (yathā Vº-Mahendrayoḥ, sc. yuddhaṃ).--§ 589 (Droṇābhishekap.): VII, 3, 93 (Śatakratum ivācintyaṃ purā Vºeṇa nirjitaṃ).--§ 592 (Saṃśaptakavadhap.): VII, 30, 1310 (Indraṃ Vºbalād iva). --§ 597 (Pratijñāp.): VII, 84, 2992 (Vāsavam iva (read with B. ºasyeva) Vºṃ hantuṃ prayāsyataḥ).--§ 599c (Jayadrathavadhap.): Formerly the Asura V. had defeated the gods; headed by Indra they then applied to Brahmán, who applied to Vishṇu, who said that Tvashṭṛ, in days of yore, had performed ascetic penances for a million of years, and that he had created V. with the permission of Maheśvara (i.e. Śiva); “you go to Śiva on the Mandara mountain.” So they did, and Śiva gave to Indra his armour cased in which Indra slew V.: VII, 94, 3457 (Daityena), 3458, 3459, 3461, 3462, 3464, 3472 (only B.), 3473 (ºcamūṃ), 3475.--§ 599 (do.): VII, 102, 3814 (jahi…yatha Vºṃ Purandaraḥ); 118, †4690 (ºendrayor yuddham iva); 124, 5008 (yathā Vºvadḥe purā).--§ 600 (Ghaṭotkacavadhap.): VII, 153, 6633 (ºhatyai yathā devāḥ parivavruḥ Purandaraṃ); 159, 7089 (yathā Vºṃ Purandaraḥ, sc. pratyudyayau); 179, †8177 (yathā Śakro Vºvadhe).--§ 603 (Nārāyaṇāstramokshap.): VII, 196, 9038 (nihate…yathā Vºe mahāsure); 200, 9323 (vajraṃ Vºe yathā Hariḥ, sc. vyasṛjat).--§ 604 (Karṇap.): VIII, 5, 143 (Mahendreṇa yathā Vºḥ, sc. nihataḥ). --§ 605 (do.): VIII, 14, 565 (yathā devāsure yuddhe VºVāsavayor iva, sc. samāgamaḥ).--§ 608 (do.): VIII, 66, †3356 (ºe hate bhagavān ivendraḥ); 67, †3375 (ºeṇa Vajrīva, sc. sametya); 69, †3398 (Marutpatir Vºm ivāttavajraḥ, sc. ahanishyat); 71, †3592 (jahi…Purandaro-Vºm iva); 73, 3675 (ºḥ prāpyeva Vāsavaṃ); 83, †4260 (ºṃ nihatyeva Sahasranetraḥ); 87, 4391 (Indra-Vºāv iva), 4406 (do.); 89, †4530 (Mahendra-Vºāv iva samprajaghnatuḥ); 91, 4798 (śiro jahāra Vºsya yathā Mahendraḥ); 94, 4948 (yathā purā Vºvadhe Śatakratuḥ); 96, 4983 (hato Vajrabhṛtā).--§ 611 (Śalyap.): IX, 17, 954 (ºvadhe yathendraṃ).--§ 613 (Gadāyuddhap.): IX, 33, 1925 (Śakro Vºm ivāhvayan).--§ 615u (Skanda): IX, 46, 2709 (jaghānāśu Vºṃ Devapatir yathā). --§ 615 (Gadāyuddhap.): IX, 55, 3128 (º-Śakrāv ivāhave); 56, 3156 (Cakro Vºm ivāhvayan); 57, 3198 (º-Vāsavayor iva, sc. yuddhaṃ), 3212 (do., do.); 58, 3251 (māyayā cākshipat tejo Vºsya Balasūdanaḥ); 61, 3395 (Indreṇeva hi Vºsya vadhaḥ), 3402 (hate).--§ 623 (Rājadh.): XII, 15, 438 (Indro Vºvadhena Mahendraḥ samapadyata).--§ 641 (do.): XII, 98, 3660 (had been slain by Indra, C. has by error Vṛttaṃ).-§ 692 (Mokshadh.): XII, 280, 10002 (Daityena), 10004 (10005), 10014; 281, (†10082), 10085, 10086, 10092 (V. was instructed by Sanatkumāra about Vishṇu).--§ 693 (do.): XII, 282, 10098, 10101 (vinihataḥ), 10102 (nirjitaḥ).-§ 693b (Vṛtravadha): XII, 282, 10104, 10106, 10107, 10109, 10110 (Asurendrasya), 10115 (dharmabhṛtāṃ varaḥ), 10116, 10117, 10118, 10126, 10127, 10128 (Daityapatiṃ), 10131, 10132 (viśvātmā); 283, 10143, 10147, 10151 (Daityaṃ), 10152, 10153, 10193, 10202 (from his blood arose śikhaṇḍas), 10204 (nihataḥ), 10207 (copious description of Indra's slaying V.).--§ 694 (Mokshadh.): XII, 284, 10208 (ºvadhāt), 10209.--§ 694b (Jvarotpatti): XII, 284, 10266 (dharmabhṛtāṃ varaḥ), 10267, 10270 (V. was overtaken with fever (cf. also § 693b), then Indra slew him with his vajra; V. ascended to the world of Vishṇu).--§ 717b (Nārāyaṇīya): XII, 343, VII), ††13213 (Tvāshṭrotpāditaṃ, the slaughter of V. narrated in accordance with § 555).--§ 719b (Gautamīlubdhaka-vyāla-Mṛtyu-Kālasaṃv.): XIII, 1, †32 (ºṃ hatvā Devarāṭ).--§ 778b (Saṃvartta-Maruttīya): XIV, 9, †246 (tridivaṃ prāg jahāra).--§ 779b (Aśvamedhikap.): In ancient times the earth was encompassed by V.; the seat of smell thus having been taken away, bad odours arose, and Indra hurled his thunderbolt at V.; V. then entered the waters, and taste thereby being taken away, Indra again hurled his thunderbolt at him. So it happened successively with light (colour and form), wind (touch) and ether (sound). Then V. suddenly entered Indra, who was then overcome with stupefaction (moha); Vasishṭha having restored him to consciousness by the Rathantara sāman, Indra in his own body slew V. with his invisible thunderbolt. This religious mystery was recited by Indra to the maharshis and by them to Kṛshṇa: XIV, 11, 298 (Indrasya saha Vºeṇa yuddhaṃ), 299, 301, 302, 304, 306, 308, 310, 311.--§ 785 (Anugītāp.): XIV, 76, 2195 (ºeṇeva Śatakratoḥ, sc. yuddhaṃ). Cf. Asura, Asuraśreshṭha, Asurendra, Daitya, Daityapati, Daityendra, Dānava, Dānavendra, Ditija, Surāri, Tvāshṭra, Viśvātman. Vṛtraśatru, Vṛtrahan, Vṛtrahantṛ, Vṛtranisūdana = Indra, q.v.

वृत्रवध
[Vṛtra-vadha(ḥ)]<BR>(“the killing of Vṛtra”): § 693b (Mokshadh.). Bhīshma said: In days of yore, Indra, accompanied by the celestial forces, proceeded in his chariot, and beheld As: Vṛtra, stationed before him like a mountain, 500 yojanas in height and 300 in circumference. Indra was struck with palsy in the lower extremities. On the eve of that great battle between D. and As. there arose loud shouts from both sides, etc. Vṛtra felt neither awe nor fear. Then the encounter commenced; the entire welking was enveloped by the combatants of both sides; all the gods with Brahmán, and Ṛ., Si., G., and Aps. on their chariots had assembled in order to see the battle. Vṛtra shot rocks, the gods arrows; Vṛtra employed illusion; Indra was stupefied; Vasishṭha restored him to his senses by the Rathantara, pointing out to him Brahmán, etc. (). Indra, by yoga, dispelled the illusions of Vṛtra. Bṛhaspati (the son of Aṅgiras) and Ṛ. repaired to Śiva, whose energy became a fever and penetrated Vṛtra; Vishṇu entered the thunderbolt of Indra; Bṛhaspati, Vasishṭha, and Ṛ. urged Indra to slay Vṛtra, and so did Śiva, saying that Vṛtra was the soul of the universe, capable of going everywhere; for 60,000 years Vṛtra had practised penances in order to obtain strength; Brahmán had given him the boons he had solicited: the greatness of yogins, large powers of illusions, excess of might, etc.; Śiva told Indra to slay him with yoga, and imparted to him his own energy. D. and Ṛ. uttered loud cheers, etc. Suddenly all As. were afflicted with the loss of memory; in a trice their powers of illusion also disappeared. Ṛ. and D. praised Śakra and Śiva (XII, 282). Description of the symptoms that appeared on the body of Vṛtra when he was overtaken by that fever; His memory issued out of his mouth in form of a dreadful jackal. Inauspicious omens. Indra looked hard at him; Vṛtra yawned and uttered superhuman cries; while Vṛtra was yawning Indra hurled his thunderbolt at him and slew him; [XII, 287, 58 ff. = 10,267 ff.: the thunderbolt cut Vṛtra into two halves; Vṛtra then came to the highest region of Vishṇu, for it was by his devotion to Vishṇu that he had overwhelmed the universe]; then Indra entered heaven with that thunderbolt pervaded by Vishṇu. Then Brahmavadhyā (description) issued out of the body of the slain Vṛtra. A little while after, when Indra was proceeding towards heaven, she seized him and stuck to him. Indra entered the fibres of a lotus stalk and dwelt there for many years. But Brahmavadhyā pursued him closely, and deprived him of all his energy. At last he repaired to Brahmán, who made her leave Indra by apportioning one quarter to Agni (whence it should enter him who did not offer oblations of seed, etc. to Agni); one quarter to the trees, herbs, and all kinds of grass (whence it should possess the men who would cut or tear any of them at parvan-days); one quarter to the Apsarases (whence it should possess that man who would cohabit with women during their courses); and one quarter to the waters (whence it should possess that man who would cast into the waters phlegm and urine and excrements). With Brahmán's permission Indra then performed a horse-sacrifice and thereby became cleansed, regained his prosperity, and slew thousands of foes. From the blood of Vṛtra were born the śikhaṇḍāḥ (the crests of cocks (?) or name of a plant, BR.), which, for this reason, must not be eaten by the twiceborn classes, etc. Blessing upon the reciter (XII, 283).

