Yudhāmanyu, a Pāñcāla, brother of Uttamaujas. § 561 (Yānasandhip.): V, 57, 2263.--§ 569 (Bhagavadyānap.): V, 141, 4779.--§ 570 (Sainyaniryāṇap.): V, 153, 5193 (devair api durāsadaḥ).--§ 572 (Rathātirathasaṅkhyānap.): V, 170, 5883 (a ratha among the Pāṇḍavas' allies).--§ 573 (Ambopākhyānap.): V, 194, 7598 (ºūttamaujasau); 195, 7624 (Pāñcālyau…Yºūttamaujasau); 196, 7638 (Pāñcālyau Yºūttamaujasau).--§ 576 (Bhagavadgītāp.): VI, 15, 606 (protects the left wheel of Arjuna); 19, 714 (Pāñcālyau Yºūttamaujasau, protectors of Arjuna's wheels); 25, 835.--§ 585 (Bhīshmavadhap.): VI, 98, 4494 (protected the left wheel of Arjuna).--§ 586 (do.): VI, 99, 4512 (only B.).--§ 587 (do.): VI, 115, 5361 (fought with Duryodhana, only C., read with B. Abhimanyuḥ).--§ 592 (Saṃśaptakavadhap.): VII, 21, 904, 911; 23, 952 (proceeded against Droṇa, description of his horses).--§ 593 (Abhimanyuvadhap.): VII, 35, 1510.--§ 597 (Pratijñāp.): VII, 83, 2951.--§ 598 (Jayadrathavadhap.): VII, 85, 3048.--§ 599 (do.): VII, 91, 3252 (cakrarakshau Pāñcālyau Yºūttamaujasau); 92, 3289, 3290; 130, 5336 (cakrarakshau tu Pāñcālyau Yºūttamaujasau), 5341, 5342, 5344, 5347 (brother of Uttamaujas), 5349, 5351 (ºūttamaujasau, defeated by Duryodhana); 137, 5629; 139, 5755 (ºo rathaṃ); 146, 6288 (only B.); 147, 6346 (cakrarakshau… Yºūttamaujasau).--§ 600 (Ghaṭotkacavadhap.): VII, 156, 6765; 177, 8059 (ºūttamaujasau); 179, 8119 (do.).-§ 604 (Karṇap.): VIII, 6, 174 (among the slain).--§ 605 (do.): VIII, 11, 428 (cakrarakshau tu Pāñcālyau Yºūttamajasau); 13, 523 (ºrathaṃ); 30, 1230.--§ 608 (do.): VIII, 59, 2946; 61, 3080 (attacked by Kṛpa), 3121, 3122 (defeated by Kṛpa); 63, 3199 (prshṭharakshau… Yºūttamaujasau); 67, †3374 (ºś cottamaujāś ca śūrau pṛshṭhato māṃ rakshatāṃ rājaputrau, says Arjuna); 75, †3811 (fought with Citrasena^8); 79, 4029; 82, †4195, †4200, †4244, †4246 (slew Citrasena^8); 96, 4991.-§ 609 (Śalyap.): IX, 1, 31 (among the slain).--§ 612 (Hradapraveśap.): IX, 30, 1726.--§ 616 (Sauptikap.): X, 8, 354 (slain by Aśvatthāman).--§ 620 (Śrāddhap.): XI, 26, 789 (his body burnt).--§ 795 (Svargārohaṇap.): XVIII, 1, 27 (bhrātarau…Yºūttamaujasau). Cf. Pāñcalaja, Pāñcālya, dual, Saumaki.

युधिष्ठिर
Yudhishṭhira Pāṇḍava, also named Ajātaśatru and Dharmarāja (Dhr.), eldest son of Pāṇḍu and Kuntī (begotten by Dharma). § 4 (Anukram.): I, 1, †109, 123, 128, †157, †170, †204 (Dhr.).--§ 11 (Parvasaṅgr.): I, 2, 395, 435, 464, 522, 534 (ºnṛpājñayā), 545 (Dhr.), 546, 593 (Dhr.), 599 (Kururājaḥ), 616 (Dhr.), 632.--§ 61 (Sarpasattra): I, 55, †2102 (yajñaḥ śruto divi devasya sūnor Yºsyājamīḍhasya).-§ 71 (Ādivaṃśāvatāraṇap.): I, 61, 2270 (Dhr., all. to § 247), 2279 (all. to Dyūtaparvan).--§ 72 (do.): I, 62, 2291 (suto Dharmasya).--§ 83 (Ādivaṃśāvatāraṇa): I, 63, 2443 (jyeshṭhaḥ), 2444 (begotten by Dharma, cf. § 190b), 2451 (begat Prativindhya on Draupadī, cf. § 253).--§ 130 (Aṃśāvat.): I, 67, 2745 (Dharmasyāṃśaṃ).--§ 157 (Pūruvaṃś.): I, 95, ††3814 (begotten by Dharma on Kuntī, cf. § 190b).--§ 159 (do.): I, 95, ††3827 (father of Prativindhya, cf. § 253), ††3828 (married Devikā, the daughter of Govāsana Śaibya, and on her begat Yaudheya).--§ 180 (Gāndhārīputrotp.): I, 115, 4506 (born before Duryodhana), 4511 (rājaputro jyeshṭhaḥ).--§ 190b (Pāṇḍavotpatti): Kuntī bore Y. [from Dharma]<BR>under the conjunction of Jyeshṭhā (Aindre candrasamāyukte) at the eighth muhūrta, named Abhijita (muhūrte 'bhijite 'shṭame) divāmadhyagate sūrye tithau pūrṇe 'tipūjite (i.e. on the fifth tithi of the bright fortnight of Āśvina, Nil.; PCR. translates: of the hour of noon of that very auspicious day of the eighth moon (Kārttika), viz. the fifth of the bright fortnight). An incorporeal voice foretold his greatness: I, 123, 4767 (Pāṇḍoḥ prathamajaḥ putraḥ). --§ 192 (do.): I, 124, 4854 (jyeshṭhaṃ, was named Y. by the brahmans).--§ 194 (Pāṇḍu): I, 126, 4919 (sākshād Dharmād ayaṃ putras tatra jāto Yºḥ).--§ 197 (Bhīmasenarasap.): I, 128, 4992, 5000; 129, 5037, 5043 (Dhr.), 5066, 5067 (Dhr.).--§ 202 (Droṇa): I, 131, 5158 (Kuntīputraḥ), (5159). --§ 205d (do.): Y. excelled everyone in driving: I, 132, 5272 (disciple of Droṇa).--§ 206 (do.): I, 132, 5280, 5281, 5283 (do.).--§ 208 (Astradarśana): I, 134, 5335 (ºpurogamāḥ, sc. the Pāṇḍavas, show their proficiency in arms); 137, †5443.--§ 209 (Drupadaśāsana): I, 138, 5467. --§ 210 (Sambhavap.): I, 139, 5517 (Pāṇḍuputraḥ, installed as yuvarāja), 5519 (Kuntīputraḥ).--§ 213 (Jatugṛhap.): I, 141, 5663; 142, 5682; 143, 5706, 5709; 145, 5746, 5747 (Dhr.), 5760 (Dhr.), (5765); 146, 5771 (Dhr.), 5780, (5781), (5787); 147, 5804 (Kuntīputraḥ), (5804); 148, 5820 (Kaunteyaḥ), (5821); 149, 5846; 150, 5871 (Kauravya); 151, 5919, 5921 (Pāṇḍavaśreshṭhaḥ) (Kuntī and the Pāṇḍavas escaped from the lac house).--§ 214 (Hiḍimbavadhap.): I, 154, 6019; 155, (6043), 6045 (Kaunteyaṃ), (6057); 156, 6095 (Dharmarāṭ) (Vyāsa ordered the Pāṇḍavas to remain in Ekacakrā).--§ 215 (Bakavadhap.): I, 162, 6242 (Pāṇḍuputraḥ), (6243), (6245), 6252, 6261; 163, (6267).--§ 219 (Caitrarathap.): I, 168, 6411, (6418) (Vyāsa ordered the Pāṇḍavas to go to the country of the Pāñcālas).--§ 221 (do.): I, 170, 6469, (6471), 6472 (Kururājaḥ) (ordered, Arjuna to spare the life of the Gandharva Citraratha).-§ 231 (Svayaṃvarap.): I, 184, (6928), (6944).--§ 233 (do.): I, 187, †7014; 188, †7058 (at the svayaṃvara of Draupadī Arjuna won her); [189, †7082 (Dharmaputraḥ)]. --§ 235 (do.): I, 191, †7133, 7145 (Kuntīputraḥ), †7150 (Ājamīḍhasya rājñaḥ).--§ 236 (do.): I, 192, †7158.--§ 237 (Vaivāhikap.): I, 193, †7195, †7202; [194, †7206 (Dhr.)]; 195, 7218, (7225) (disclosed to Drupada that they were the Pāṇḍavas), 7230, 7231, 7234 (Kuntīputraṃ), 7238, (7240) (asked Drupada to give Draupadī to the Pāṇḍavas as their common wife), (7246); 196, (7264), 7269.--§ 240 (do.): I, 198, †7339; 199, 7365 (Dhr.) (Y. and his brothers married Draupadī).--§ 241 (Vidurāgamanap.): I, 201, 7401 (Kuntīputraṃ).--§ 244 (Rājyalābhap.): I, 207, 7547 (Kuntīputraḥ), (7549), 7553 (Pāṇḍuputraḥ) (Y. became king in Indraprastha).--§ 245 (do.): I, 208, 7601, 7604, 7605, 7607, 7611, 7615, (7616).--§ 246 (Sundopasundop.): I, 209, 7619 (Pārtha) (Nārada told Y. and his brothers the story of Sunda and Upasunda, then t